WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.560
The bloodstream is an extremely
dramatic place. It links all your

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.090
organs together, provides nutrients,
proteins, and oxygen. About 60% of the

3
00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.850
five liters of blood in your body is a
fluid called plasma. The rest is cells,

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.870
and it's all moving at three to four
miles per hour. These cells transform

5
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.970
into fierce warriors whenever an
invader is detected. Introducing your

6
00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.180
body's supporters and defenders: the
cells of the blood.

7
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.240
Our journey begins in the bone marrow,
the spongy tissue inside our bones

8
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.530
where the blood cells are born. They
escape through little vessels that

9
00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.990
connect the bones to the rest of the
bloodstream. There are three main types

10
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:41.220
of cells in the blood, each with a
specialized role: platelets, red blood

11
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.550
cells and white blood cells. Let's take
a closer look. First up, we have

12
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:51.160
platelets, tiny little helpers that
flow through the bloodstream, not doing

13
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.830
a whole lot. But when a vessel gets
damaged, either from a chemical

14
00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.250
imbalance in the surrounding area or
from physical damage like a cut,

15
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.540
platelets get a signal that it's their
time to shine. They rush to the site of

16
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.380
damage and stick together, extending
little tentacles to patch up any holes

17
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.270
in the vessel, forming a clot and
stopping more damage. Next up, our red

18
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.020
blood cells, also called erythrocytes.

19
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.180
They're the most abundant blood cell.
In every cubic millimeter of blood you

20
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.210
can find around five million red blood
cells. Their job is to pick up oxygen

21
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.910
from the lungs, stored in little red
packages called hemoglobin that make

22
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.160
the blood red. Then they truck it to
the rest of the body so your organs can

23
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.750
use it to make energy from sugar and
fat. They're so dedicated to this job

24
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.910
that they don't even have nuclei. Their
dented hockey puck shape makes more

25
00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.920
room for hemoglobin. After they drop
off their oxygen, they have to return

26
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.060
to the lungs to grab more and start the
process all over again. The most

27
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.900
complicated cells of the blood are the
white blood cells, a.k.a., leukocytes.

28
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.190
An important part of the body's immune
system, they're a squadron of elite

29
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.020
agents that identify and hunt down
threats to the body, like viruses and

30
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.850
bacteria. They can be divided by their
specialized roles. Some cells notice

31
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.970
when something's off and sound the
alarm. Others are in charge of directly

32
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.060
fighting the threat. These cells can
recognize, remember and hunt down virus

33
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.030
infected cells, mutated tumor cells,
you name it. And those are the cells of

34
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:18.550
the blood.

